<TopicPage slug="subjects/political-science/intermediate/constitutions-rights">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A constitution is a country's operating system: the rules about making rules. It says who may
    exercise power, through which offices, under what limits — and, crucially, it claims to stand
    <em>above</em> ordinary politics, so that the winners of any one election cannot simply
    rewrite the game in their own favour. Its two core moves are <strong>separating power</strong>
    so that no person or body holds all of it, and <strong>entrenching rights</strong> so that
    majorities cannot casually steamroll minorities. Nearly every state now has a written
    constitution; the United Kingdom is the famous holdout, governing instead by accumulation —
    and comparing the two models reveals what a constitution is actually <em>for</em>, as opposed
    to what it looks like on paper.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from Montesquieu to judicial review — five steps of restrained power" widget="equationStep" config={sepCfg} />

  <h2>What a constitution consists of — and how it is guarded</h2>
  <p>
    Two classifications matter more than the paper question. First, <strong>written versus
    unwritten</strong>. Written constitutions (USA 1787, India 1950, South Africa 1996) are
    authoritative documents; "unwritten" ones like the UK's are really <em>uncodified</em> — a
    mix of statute law (the Parliament Acts, the Human Rights Act 1998), common law principles,
    authoritative works of authority (Bagehot, Erskine May), historical documents (Magna Carta
    1215, the Bill of Rights 1689) and <strong>conventions</strong>: binding-in-practice rules no
    court could enforce, such as the monarch granting royal assent to bills passed by Parliament.
    Conventions are constitution-making by good faith — powerful precisely because everyone
    treats them as binding. Second, <strong>rigid versus flexible</strong>: how hard is amendment?
    The US Constitution requires supermajorities in two-thirds of states and both chambers — 27
    amendments in over 235 years (and its most important, the 14th, rewrote citizenship after a
    civil war). India's, amended over a hundred times, sits at the flexible end. Flexibility
    trades agility against stability; rigidity does the reverse. The UK is maximally flexible —
    a simple Act of Parliament can change anything, which makes the constitution cheap to fix and
    cheap to break.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> an unwritten constitution means no constitutional limits.
  The UK's limits are real but <em>political</em> rather than legal: they hold because actors
  expect each other to respect them. That works until someone is willing to absorb the norms-breaking
  costs — which is exactly the scenario the flexible model has no lock against.</p>

  <h2>Separation of powers: the machinery of mutual suspicion</h2>
  <p>
    Montesquieu's 1748 argument was empirical, not utopian: everywhere he looked, liberty died
    wherever lawmaking, enforcement and judging belonged to the same hands. The remedy was
    structural — a legislature to write the law, an executive to enforce it, a judiciary to
    interpret it, each staffed differently, elected or appointed differently, and given
    <strong>checking powers</strong> over the others: presidential veto, legislative override,
    confirmation of judges, impeachment, court review of statutes. The design philosophy is
    stark: <em>ambition must be made to counteract ambition</em> (Madison, Federalist 51) —
    assume office-holders will grab power, and arrange things so that each grab triggers another
    branch's self-interested resistance. Deadlock, the designers concluded, is a feature: it is
    what stopping a determined faction looks like from the inside.
  </p>
  <p>
    Real systems bend the theory. Parliamentary systems (UK, Germany, Japan) deliberately
    <em>fuse</em> legislature and executive — the prime minister sits in Parliament and commands
    its majority — while concentrating the separation in an independent judiciary and fixed
    terms. Presidential systems keep all three formally separate at the cost of "divided
    government" standoffs. Neither design is superior on paper; both depend, in practice, on the
    actors' willingness to stay inside their lanes. Where the lanes are respected out of habit,
    constitutions need few locks; where they are not, no number of locks has yet proven
    sufficient by itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Judicial review: the courts as referees</h2>
  <p>
    The boldest institutional invention in this tradition is <strong>judicial review</strong> —
    the power of courts to void acts that contradict the constitution. It was not granted
    explicitly in the US document; Chief Justice John Marshall simply claimed it in
    <em>Marbury v. Madison</em> (1803), reasoning that a law contrary to the constitution "is
    void", and that it is the judiciary's province "to say what the law is". Two centuries later
    the power exists in some form on every continent. Its variants matter. <strong>Strong
    review</strong> (US, Germany, Canada, South Africa) lets courts strike down legislation;
    the German Federal Constitutional Court, built after 1949 explicitly as a "militant
    democracy", can ban parties hostile to the constitutional order. <strong>Weak
    review</strong> preserves legislative sovereignty: under the UK's Human Rights Act 1998,
    courts may <em>declare</em> a statute incompatible with the European Convention on Human
    Rights — a public embarrassment with no automatic legal effect, leaving Parliament to amend
    or not.
  </p>
  <p>
    The UK's own court architecture was reformed remarkably late. Before 2009 the highest
    appellate court was a committee of the House of Lords — Law Lords legislating by day and
    judging the same disputes. The Constitutional Reform Act 2005 separated those roles and
    created the UK Supreme Court (open for business 2009). Its first great test came quickly:
    in <em>Miller (2017)</em> it ruled that triggering Brexit required an Act of Parliament, and
    in <em>Miller II (2019)</em> that the Prime Minister's five-week prorogation of Parliament
    was unlawful and void — both rulings enforcing, not overriding, parliamentary sovereignty.
    Note the direction: in a flexible constitution the courts' main practical job is policing the
    <em>executive</em>, via judicial review of administrative action — checking that ministers
    acted within their legal powers, followed fair procedure, and reached rational decisions.
    Administrative law, not rights litigation, is the workaday face of judicial power.
  </p>

  <h2>Rights in context: what rights actually do</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Negative versus positive.</strong> Most classic rights are "thou shalt not"
    constraints on the state: free speech, religion, fair trial, freedom from arbitrary
    detention. They cost little to respect. Newer social rights are obligations to provide —
    education, healthcare, housing — and they cost money, which is why constitutions treat them
    differently (South Africa's entrenches both; its Constitutional Court's 2002 Treatment
    Action Campaign ruling forced the state to extend anti-HIV medication, a landmark in
    justiciable social rights). <strong>Rights also collide</strong>: speech against defamation
    and incitement, protest against public order, privacy against press freedom. Courts resolve
    collisions with a <strong>proportionality</strong> test: is the limitation prescribed by law,
    pursuing a legitimate aim, necessary, and the least restrictive means available? Notice what
    this makes of rights language: not absolute trump cards, but strong presumptions that the
    state must justify overriding — a speech ban for a compelling, narrow reason survives; a
    blanket one does not.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Who do rights bind?</strong> Primarily the state — that is the point of the
    architecture. Rights are promises made <em>to</em> the losers of elections: you may hold your
    views, publish your criticisms, worship as you choose, and take the government to court,
    because a different majority will govern tomorrow and it will inherit the same limits.
    Bills of rights — America's (1791), South Africa's, the UN Universal Declaration (1948) —
    set limits no current majority may cross for the comfort of its supporters. Majorities are
    not always right; that is the point.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The international floor.</strong> The 1948 Universal Declaration is a declaration,
    not a treaty, but it seeded binding covenants in 1966 (on civil-political and
    economic-social rights), regional courts (the European Court of Human Rights at Strasbourg,
    hearing cases from 46 states against their own governments), and the vocabulary every modern
    constitution borrows. Enforcement remains the weak link — declarations bind reputations more
    reliably than armies — but the normative floor now exists, and domestic constitutional
    courts cite it constantly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> constitutional rights are absolute. Every functioning rights
  system contains limits — you cannot use speech to defraud, and even the US First Amendment
  yields to true threats and incitement. The real contest is never "rights or no rights" but
  <em>who decides</em> the limits, under what test, and how easily the limit expands in a panic.
  Wartime is the recurring stress case: Japanese-American internment (upheld 1944 in
  <em>Korematsu</em>, formally repudiated by the Supreme Court in 2018 as gravely wrong) remains
  the standard warning of what fear does to paper guarantees.</p>

  <h2>Devolution and the question of sovereignty</h2>
  <p>
    Since 1997 the UK constitution has been rebuilt in layers without ever being codified:
    devolved parliaments in Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland (with tax-varying powers in
    Scotland that are now substantial), the Human Rights Act, House of Lords reform, and
    referendums as a new decision device. Each layer raised the same theoretical question the
    syllabus keeps returning to: <em>where does sovereignty actually live now?</em> The
    textbook answer — Westminster Parliament, sovereign, may legislate on anything — survives
    formally (devolution is legally revocable; the European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018
    repossessed the powers Brexit returned), but politically Westminster can no longer exercise
    unchecked power over devolved matters without a legitimacy crisis. A constitution can be
    formally unchanged and practically transformed; the gap between the two is where
    constitutional conflict now happens.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Constitutional economics.</strong> amendment difficulty is measurable. Kim and colleagues'
    comparative work finds "amendment difficulty" correlates with longevity but also with
    obsolescence risk: the US document's near-untouchability (no successful amendment since 1971)
    pushes change out of Article V and into court interpretation — arguably transferring
    constitution-making from the people's representatives to nine judges. Comparative
    constitutional design has noticed: newer constitutions (South Africa, Kenya 2010) add
    sunset clauses, mandatory review periods, and easier amendment thresholds for ordinary
    provisions while entrenching core values more deeply.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The warning label.</strong> Paper constraints fail when the actors agree to ignore
    them, and the failure is rarely a dramatic coup. Political scientists (Levitsky and Ziblatt's
    <em>How Democracies Die</em> is the standard text) watch for the soft attacks: court
    packing — adding loyal judges until the referee says whatever the government wants (FDR's
    1937 plan, Hungary and Poland's 2010s rebuilds); capturing referees such as electoral
    commissions; constitutional hardball — changing rules after each election, rushing bills
    through emptied chambers, refusing hearings. Each move is technically legal; together they
    hollow the constitution. The UK's flexible model is exposed to all of it: prorogation in
    2019 was a legal-nullification episode in real time.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> Should courts strike down laws at all — is judicial review
    "counter-majoritarian difficulty" (Bickel's phrase) or the rights of the minority operating
    as designed? Does a bill of rights without a rights-respecting culture decay into decoration?
    Can international courts like Strasbourg coexist with national sovereignty, or does the
    "dialogue" model (parliament responds, court replies) hide a one-way ratchet? And can
    constitutions designed for the printing-press state adapt to platforms, algorithms and
    emergencies announced by tweet? No settled answers — but every generation since 1689 has
    had to re-answer the same question the age of revolutions left us: <em>who guards the
    guardians, and what happens when they stop?</em>
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Montesquieu, The Spirit of the Laws (1748); Madison, Federalist No. 51; Marbury v. Madison (1803); Miller v. Secretary of State (2017, 2019); UN Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948); Levitsky & Ziblatt, How Democracies Die; House of Commons Library briefings on the UK constitution.</p>
